consiSt("va",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of Virginia","d":"Court of last resort. Since January 1, 2022 it hears civil appeals mainly by discretionary petition for appeal from the Court of Appeals (Code §§ 8.01-670, 17.1-411); appeals still lie directly to it from habeas corpus decisions, collateral attacks on criminal convictions, the State Corporation Commission, and attorney-discipline proceedings under §§ 54.1-3935 and 54.1-3937 (Code § 17.1-406(B)). It also decides 15-day petitions for review of preliminary-injunction orders, pretrial immunity rulings, and (since 2026) court-closure orders (Code §§ 8.01-626, 8.01-670.2, 8.01-670.3), adopts the Rules of Court, and administers pro hac vice records. All filings except the record go through the VACES e-filing system (Rule 5:1B)."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Court of Appeals of Virginia","d":"Hears appeals of right from any final judgment of a circuit court in a civil matter (other than the § 17.1-406(B) categories), from administrative-agency appeals decided by a circuit court, from the Virginia Workers’ Compensation Commission, and from criminal convictions, plus listed interlocutory orders (equitable orders requiring payment or adjudicating the principles of a cause, and § 8.01-675.5 certified questions by permission) (Code §§ 17.1-405, 17.1-406(A)). Sits in three-judge panels with en banc rehearing; e-filing through VACES is mandatory for lawyers (Rule 5A:1(c))."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Circuit Courts","d":"General-jurisdiction courts of record. Civil practice is governed by Parts One, Three and Four of the Rules of Supreme Court of Virginia and Title 8.01 of the Code. Electronic filing is available only in courts that have set up a system, and a civil case becomes an Electronically Filed Case only with all parties’ consent (Rules 1:17, 3:3). Circuit courts may adopt local rules, which must be entered in the order book, posted and filed with the Executive Secretary; a lawyer appearing in a circuit where the lawyer does not regularly practice is responsible for learning and following them (Rule 1:15(a))."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"General District Courts","d":"Courts not of record for smaller civil claims, traffic and misdemeanor cases, governed by Parts Seven A–C of the Rules. A party who timely perfects an appeal to circuit court may take a nonsuit there, which annuls the district court judgment (Code § 8.01-380(F)). Jurisdictional amount limits are statutory and were not collected."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Juvenile and Domestic Relations District Courts","d":"Courts not of record for juvenile, family, custody, support and protective-order matters, governed by Part Eight of the Rules. Rule 1:5(f)(5) allows a qualified legal services lawyer a one-hearing limited appearance in these courts and in general district court."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Virginia’s anti-SLAPP law is an immunity statute, not a special motion to strike: Code § 8.01-223.2","d":"A person is immune from tort liability if the claim is based solely on statements (i) on matters of public concern that would be protected by the First Amendment and are communicated to a third party; (ii) made at a public hearing before, or otherwise communicated to, a local governing body or its boards, commissions, agencies and authorities on matters properly before it; (iii) made at a Title IX hearing at a college or university; or (iv) made by an employee against an employer where retaliation for the statement is barred by § 40.1-27.3.\nThe immunity does not cover statements the speaker knew or should have known were false, or made with reckless disregard for whether they were false.\nThe history line lists amendments in 2016, 2017, 2020, 2023 (cc. 462, 463) and 2025 (c. 641). Which clause each amendment added was not checked against the session laws; the text quoted is the current Code as displayed on November 10, 2026.","c":"Va. Code § 8.01-223.2(A)–(B)","v":true},{"t":"No special motion, no discovery stay, no statutory deadline","d":"The statute sets out no special motion to strike, no filing deadline, no automatic stay of discovery, and no expedited hearing. The immunity has to be raised through ordinary Virginia procedure, for example a demurrer, plea in bar, or summary-judgment motion (Rules 3:8, 3:20), or a motion to quash a subpoena. Keep in mind that Rule 3:20 bars summary judgment based on discovery depositions unless an exception applies.","c":"Va. Code § 8.01-223.2; Rules 3:8, 3:20","v":true},{"t":"Fees: discretionary award to the prevailing defendant or witness","d":"A person who has a suit dismissed, has a witness subpoena or subpoena duces tecum quashed, or otherwise prevails under the immunity “may be awarded” reasonable attorney fees and costs. The award is discretionary, not mandatory. Under Rule 3:25, a fee claim generally has to be demanded in a pleading; whether that rule reaches a defendant’s § 8.01-223.2 fee request was not resolved from the collected text, so plead it.","c":"Va. Code § 8.01-223.2(C); Rule 3:25","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: no interlocutory appeal specific to anti-SLAPP rulings","d":"The collected statutes give no interlocutory appeal for the denial of § 8.01-223.2 immunity. The 15-day petition for review under § 8.01-670.2 covers only pleas of sovereign, absolute, or qualified immunity that would spare the movant from participating in the case. Whether § 8.01-223.2 immunity counts as one of those was not determined from the collected text; otherwise review comes after final judgment, or by certified interlocutory appeal under § 8.01-675.5.","c":"Va. Code §§ 8.01-670.2, 8.01-675.5","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice under Rule 1A:4: Virginia local counsel must sign everything and attend everything","d":"An out-of-state lawyer currently licensed in another U.S. state, territory or possession may appear in a particular case before any Virginia court, board or agency only after associating with an active Virginia State Bar member as local counsel. Local counsel’s name must appear on all filed documents; local counsel must personally appear and take part in every pretrial conference, hearing and trial; and local counsel shares joint responsibility for the case. Any pleading or paper that must be served is invalid unless local counsel signs it. Service on local counsel counts as service on the out-of-state lawyer.","c":"Rule 1A:4(1)–(2)","v":true},{"t":"Application: notarized form, $250 per case, motion by local counsel","d":"The out-of-state lawyer gives local counsel an original notarized application on the Supreme Court’s form and a non-refundable $250 fee, payable to the Clerk of the Supreme Court of Virginia, for each separate case. Local counsel files a motion to associate with proof of service, the application, a proposed order and the fee. The out-of-state lawyer may not appear until the order is entered, although the tribunal may allow temporary admission for good cause with a deadline to finish the application. Admission lasts until the case ends in Virginia courts and carries over on transfer or appeal, subject to revocation.","c":"Rule 1A:4(3)(a)–(c), (4), (6)","v":true},{"t":"Twelve-case limit in 12 months","d":"The tribunal should deny the motion if the lawyer has been admitted pro hac vice in 12 cases anywhere in Virginia in the 12 months before the application. It may take related or consolidated matters into account, and it checks the count with the Supreme Court before ruling. Admitted lawyers must report disciplinary developments promptly and are subject to Virginia discipline as if they were bar members.","c":"Rule 1A:4(5), (7), (9)","v":true},{"t":"Appellate argument also needs Rule 1A:4 admission","d":"A lawyer not licensed in Virginia who wants to present oral argument in the Court of Appeals must comply with Rule 1A:4.","c":"Rule 5A:28(c)","v":true},{"t":"Work allowed without pro hac vice admission","d":"Subject to Rule 5.5 of the Virginia Rules of Professional Conduct, an out-of-state lawyer may, without a pro hac vice order: work in Virginia on a proceeding pending elsewhere in which the lawyer is admitted; if the lawyer reasonably expects to qualify for pro hac vice, consult with Virginia counsel, talk with a prospective client about retention, and prepare a potential Virginia case; and prepare for and take part in ADR wherever it takes place. Under RPC 5.5(d)(3) a “Foreign Lawyer” must tell the client and interested third parties in writing that the lawyer is not admitted in Virginia, name the jurisdictions where the lawyer is licensed, and give the lawyer’s office address there.","c":"Rule 1A:4(10); Va. RPC 5.5(d)(1)–(4)","v":true},{"t":"Other routes: admission without exam (amended effective January 8, 2027), corporate counsel, military spouses","d":"Part One A also covers admission without examination for experienced lawyers from reciprocal jurisdictions (Rule 1A:1), Virginia corporate counsel and corporate counsel registrants (Rule 1A:5), military legal assistance attorneys (1A:6), foreign legal consultants (1A:7), military servicemembers and spouses (1A:8), and legal aid counsel (1A:9). An Order of November 9, 2026 amends Rule 1A:1 effective January 8, 2027. The amended introduction requires five years’ admission before a court of last resort, admission in a Reciprocal Jurisdiction, and active practice (at least 20 hours a week) in at least three of the last five years. Rule 1A:8 was amended several times in 2025–2026 (an order of July 17, 2026 was vacated September 4, 2026). Check the current text before applying.","c":"Rules 1A:1 (as amended eff. Jan. 8, 2027), 1A:5–1A:9","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances","d":"A lawyer employed by a qualified legal services provider, or acting pro bono on a direct referral from one, may file and serve a notice of limited scope appearance in any civil proceeding. Any other lawyer needs leave of court. During the limited appearance, papers must be served on both the lawyer and the party. The lawyer leaves by filing a notice of completion endorsed by the client, or by a motion to terminate with 7 days for objection. A single-hearing notice is available in general district and J&DR courts. Ordinary withdrawal requires leave after notice to the client, and the order must name substitute counsel or give the client’s contact details.","c":"Rule 1:5(d), (f)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing access: circuit-court portal remote access is limited to Virginia State Bar members","d":"Remote electronic access to documents in an Electronically Filed Case is limited to judges and court staff, active members of the Virginia State Bar and their authorized agents, and registered pro se parties. Pro hac vice counsel should plan to work through local counsel’s account. Appellate filings go through VACES, where lawyers must e-file in PDF and electronically serve opposing counsel (Rules 5:1B, 5A:1(c)). The first pleading in a court of record must carry the attorney’s Virginia State Bar number, email and telephone number (Code § 8.01-271.1(A)).","c":"Rule 1:17(c)(5); Rules 5:1B, 5A:1(c); Va. Code § 8.01-271.1(A)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Virginia Rules of Professional Conduct (Rules of Court, Part Six, § II)","d":"The Rules of Professional Conduct are Part Six, § II of the Rules of Supreme Court of Virginia, but the Supreme Court’s compilation points to the Virginia State Bar’s website for the text. The collected Bar compilation is dated July 27, 2026 (reflecting the Rule 1.9 amendment effective that day). The rules follow ABA Model Rules numbering, but many ABA comments are marked “not adopted”, and each rule carries a “Virginia Code Comparison” to the former Code of Professional Responsibility.","c":"Rules of Court Part Six, § II; Va. RPC (VSB compilation July 27, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality: narrower definition, and mandatory disclosure of some client crimes","d":"Rule 1.6(a) protects privileged information and other information gained in the representation that the client asked to keep confidential or whose disclosure would embarrass or likely harm the client. That is narrower than the ABA’s “information relating to the representation”. Rule 1.6(c) requires the lawyer to promptly reveal a client’s stated intention to commit a crime reasonably certain to cause death, substantial bodily harm, or substantial injury to another’s financial interests or property, after first warning the client where feasible. Permissive disclosure includes information that clearly establishes the client committed a fraud on a third party during the representation (1.6(b)(3)).","c":"Va. RPC 1.6(a)–(c)","v":true},{"t":"Fees: nonrefundable advance fees are banned; contingent-fee writing need not be client-signed","d":"Rule 1.5(g) prohibits nonrefundable advanced legal fees. A contingent-fee agreement must “state in writing” the method, percentages for settlement, trial or appeal, and how expenses are handled. The text does not require the client’s signature, unlike ABA Model Rule 1.5(c). Fee division between lawyers in different firms requires client consent to the participation of all lawyers and to the terms of the division, obtained in advance, preferably in writing. There is no proportion-to-services or joint-responsibility condition, and 1.5(f) exempts lawyers formerly in the same firm and successive counsel.","c":"Va. RPC 1.5(c), (e)–(g)","v":true},{"t":"No screening to cure lateral-hire conflicts","d":"Virginia Rule 1.10(a) imputes a lawyer’s Rule 1.6, 1.7, 1.9 or 2.10(e) conflict to everyone in the firm. Unlike ABA Model Rule 1.10(a)(2), it has no exception for a personally disqualified lateral lawyer who is timely screened; the remedy is waiver by the affected client under Rule 1.7 (1.10(c)). Screening appears only for former government lawyers (1.10(e), 1.11).","c":"Va. RPC 1.10(a), (c), (e)","v":true},{"t":"Former clients: 2026 amendment on criminal-history information","d":"Rule 1.9(c)(1) bars using a former client’s information to the former client’s disadvantage. The exceptions are what Rules 1.6 or 3.3 allow, information that has become generally known, and information that consists solely of criminal history obtainable from law-enforcement databases or court records. The compilation is marked effective July 27, 2026 for Rule 1.9. That the criminal-history clause is the July 2026 change was inferred and not confirmed against the amendment order.","c":"Va. RPC 1.9(c)(1)","v":true},{"t":"Threatening criminal or disciplinary charges to gain a civil advantage is prohibited","d":"Rule 3.4(i) forbids presenting or threatening to present criminal or disciplinary charges solely to obtain an advantage in a civil matter. The ABA Model Rules have no comparable black-letter rule. Rule 3.4(j) separately bars filing suit or taking other action that the lawyer knows, or that is obvious, would serve merely to harass or maliciously injure.","c":"Va. RPC 3.4(i)–(j)","v":true},{"t":"Misconduct: no ABA 8.4(g); bar on agreements limiting bar complaints","d":"Virginia Rule 8.4 has no counterpart to ABA Model Rule 8.4(g) on harassment or discrimination. Rule 8.4(b) reaches criminal or “deliberately wrongful” acts reflecting on honesty or fitness, and 8.4(c) reaches dishonesty, fraud, deceit or misrepresentation that reflects adversely on fitness to practice. Rule 8.4(f), effective March 4, 2024 per the amendment list, forbids agreements limiting a client’s or former client’s right to file or pursue a bar complaint.","c":"Va. RPC 8.4(a)–(f)","v":true},{"t":"Trial publicity rule covers only criminal jury matters","d":"Rule 3.6 restricts extrajudicial statements only by lawyers involved in the investigation, prosecution or defense of a criminal matter that may be tried by a jury. The standard is a statement the lawyer knows or should know will have a substantial likelihood of interfering with the fairness of the jury trial. Unlike ABA Model Rule 3.6, it does not reach civil cases.","c":"Va. RPC 3.6","v":true},{"t":"Mediator and third-party neutral rules not in the ABA set","d":"Virginia keeps Rules 2.10 (third-party neutral) and 2.11 (mediator), which the Committee adopted outside the ABA Model Rules, alongside Rule 2.4. Rule 2.2 (intermediary) is also retained.","c":"Va. RPC 2.2, 2.4, 2.10, 2.11","v":true}],"style":"Commonwealth of Virginia","home":"https://www.vacourts.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Post-trial motions do not extend the 21-day finality window or the 30-day appeal clock","d":"A final judgment leaves the trial court’s control 21 days after the judge signs it. Filing a motion for a new trial or reconsideration does not extend that time or the time to note an appeal. Only an order that actually modifies, vacates or suspends the judgment within the 21 days does, and appeal time then runs from the new final judgment. Get a suspending order entered, not just a motion filed.","c":"Rule 1:1(a); Rule 5A:3(a)"},{"t":"Notice of appeal goes to the trial court clerk within 30 days, with the $50 fee and an appeal bond","d":"The notice of appeal to the Court of Appeals must be filed with the clerk of the circuit court within 30 days after entry of the final judgment. A copy goes to the Court of Appeals clerk with the $50 fee (the appeal is dismissed if the fee does not arrive within 10 days). In an appeal of right in a civil case, a $500 appeal bond (or the amount the trial court sets) must be filed simultaneously. The bond is not jurisdictional, but missing it invites a motion.","c":"Rule 5A:6(a), (c); Va. Code §§ 8.01-675.3, 8.01-676.1(A), (P)"},{"t":"Transcripts are due in the trial court within 60 days of judgment","d":"A transcript becomes part of the record only if filed in the circuit court clerk’s office within 60 days after entry of final judgment. The Court of Appeals can extend that only on a written motion filed within 90 days after entry. Notice of filing the transcript is due to all counsel within 10 days. Without needed transcripts, the assignments of error may not be reachable.","c":"Rule 5A:8(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Preliminary assignments of error are due 15 days after the record is filed","d":"In an appeal of right, the appellant must file a preliminary statement of assignments of error within 15 days after the record is filed in the Court of Appeals, and the appellee files any preliminary assignments of cross-error within 10 days after that. The opening brief is due 40 days after the record is filed. Assignments of error must point to exact record pages where each error was preserved; a bare “contrary to the law and the evidence” assignment is insufficient.","c":"Rules 5A:19(b)(1)–(2), 5A:20, 5A:18"},{"t":"Preserve every objection with reasonable certainty at the time of the ruling","d":"No ruling will be considered as a basis for reversal unless an objection was stated with reasonable certainty at the time, except for good cause or to attain the ends of justice. A general statement that the judgment is contrary to the law and the evidence preserves nothing.","c":"Rule 5A:18; Rule 5:25"},{"t":"Pro hac vice papers are invalid without local counsel’s signature","d":"Any pleading or paper that must be served, including discovery, is invalid unless signed by Virginia local counsel. Local counsel must personally attend every proceeding. The first pleading in a court of record must give a Virginia State Bar number, and a signature by anyone other than an active VSB member or pro se party is not valid. A signature defect must be cured within 21 days after it is raised, or the paper is stricken.","c":"Rule 1A:4(2); Va. Code § 8.01-271.1(A), (G)"},{"t":"Jury demand within 10 days after the last pleading","d":"A party who wants a jury must demand it in the complaint or serve and file a written demand within 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue. Amended pleadings do not revive the right unless the order says so. A defaulting defendant waives jury trial.","c":"Rules 3:21(b), (d); 3:19(a)"},{"t":"No summary judgment on discovery depositions","d":"A motion for summary judgment or to strike the evidence cannot be sustained if based in whole or in part on discovery depositions under Rule 4:5. The exceptions are all parties agreeing, or the motion coming within § 8.01-420(B)–(C), which allows depositions and affidavits when the only parties are business entities and $50,000 or more is at issue. Summary judgment is decided on the pleadings, pretrial orders and admissions.","c":"Rule 3:20; Va. Code § 8.01-420"},{"t":"Answer in 21 days, no general denial, no serial motions","d":"Responsive pleadings are due 21 days after service (60 days after a timely waiver request, 90 if sent outside Virginia). Answers must respond paragraph by paragraph. After the first response, further demurrers, pleas or motions need leave of court. The time to move for a change of venue cannot be extended except as § 8.01-264 allows.","c":"Rules 3:8(a)–(b), 1:9"},{"t":"Leave to amend from the bench starts a 21-day clock that can end the case","d":"There is no amendment as of right. When leave is granted other than on a written motion (for example, when a demurrer is sustained with leave to amend), the amended pleading is due within 21 days unless the court sets another time. If it is not filed in time, the demurrer order becomes a final disposition of the claim and the 30-day appeal clock can start running.","c":"Rules 1:8, 1:1(c)"},{"t":"Fee claims must be pleaded","d":"Except for § 8.01-271.1 sanctions and certain domestic-relations fees, a party seeking attorney fees must demand them in a complaint, counterclaim, cross-claim, third-party claim or responsive pleading, stating the basis. Otherwise the claim is waived unless leave to amend is granted.","c":"Rule 3:25"},{"t":"Nonsuit is a right, but only once and only before submission","d":"A plaintiff may take one nonsuit as of right before a motion to strike is sustained, the jury retires, or the case is submitted to the court. The plaintiff may then refile within six months or the remaining limitations period, whichever is longer, generally in the same court. Defendants should expect it: expert fees can be shifted only if notice comes within 7 days of trial, and a nonsuit cannot defeat a related counterclaim that cannot stand alone.","c":"Va. Code §§ 8.01-380(A)–(D), 8.01-229(E)(3)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory substitution of judge; recusal under Canon 1D","d":"The collected Rules of Court provide no peremptory challenge or automatic substitution of a trial judge. A judge must recuse in any proceeding in which the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned. Listed examples include personal bias or personal knowledge of disputed facts, prior service as a lawyer in the matter, having presided over it in another court, an economic interest of the judge or listed family members, and relationships within the third degree to a party, lawyer or likely material witness. A judge may recuse without stating the basis on the record. The rule of necessity can override disqualification.\nThe Canons provide no motion procedure, filing deadline or reassignment mechanism; those come from statute and case law, which were not collected.","c":"Canons of Judicial Conduct, Canon 1D(1)–(6)","v":true},{"t":"Remittal of disqualification","d":"For any ground other than personal bias or prejudice concerning a party, the judge may have the parties and lawyers consider waiver outside the judge’s presence. If all agree and the judge is willing, the judge may sit, and the written agreement goes into the record. The identity of anyone who declines to waive is not to be told to the judge. Disclosure of past professional associations ended more than one year earlier, or past clients represented more than three years earlier, is presumptively not required.","c":"Canon 1D(4), (9)","v":true},{"t":"Canon 1 amendment pending","d":"The Supreme Court’s amendment list shows an order amending Canon 1 effective December 7, 2026. That order was not collected; check it before relying on Canon 1D.","c":"Supreme Court of Virginia, Amendment Orders page (Canon 1, eff. Dec. 7, 2026)","v":false}],"speech":[{"t":"Immediate Supreme Court review of court-closure orders (2026)","d":"New Code § 8.01-670.3 (2026, c. 366) makes immediately reviewable any circuit court order that grants or denies a motion to close a proceeding not otherwise authorized to be closed, closes one on its own motion, or affirms or reverses a district court’s closure order. Any aggrieved person may petition the Supreme Court within 15 days under § 8.01-626. Rule 1:1C, amended effective September 15, 2026, gives the appellate court exclusive jurisdiction over the order under review. Amended Rule 4:15(b1), effective January 8, 2027, will require advance notice of any intended closure motion, and other counsel must give notice at least 2 days before the hearing.","c":"Va. Code §§ 8.01-670.3, 8.01-626; Rule 1:1C; Rule 4:15(b1) (eff. Jan. 8, 2027)","v":true},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP immunity extends to Title IX hearings and protected employee statements","d":"Beyond public-concern speech and statements to local governing bodies, § 8.01-223.2 immunity covers statements made at a Title IX hearing at an institution of higher education and statements by an employee against an employer where retaliation is barred by § 40.1-27.3. Statements made with knowledge of falsity or reckless disregard are not covered.","c":"Va. Code § 8.01-223.2(A)(iii)–(iv), (B)","v":true},{"t":"Lawyer speech: trial publicity limits apply only in criminal jury matters","d":"Virginia Rule 3.6 does not restrict lawyers’ public statements about civil cases; it reaches only criminal matters that may be tried by a jury.","c":"Va. RPC 3.6","v":true}],"other":[{"t":"Civil procedure is split between the Rules of Court and Title 8.01","d":"Pleading, discovery and motion practice are in Parts One, Three and Four of the Rules of Supreme Court of Virginia. Nonsuit, limitations and tolling, service timing, signature requirements, sanctions, appeal bonds and appellate jurisdiction are statutory (Title 8.01 and Title 17.1). Virginia uses demurrers, pleas in bar and motions craving oyer rather than Federal Rule 12 motions.","c":"Rules 3:8, 1:4; Va. Code §§ 8.01-271.1, 8.01-275.1, 8.01-380, 8.01-676.1","v":true},{"t":"Rules in flux: orders effective January 8, 2027","d":"An Order of November 9, 2026 amends Rules 4:12 (new sanctions for failing to supplement discovery unless substantially justified or harmless), 4:15 (closure-motion notice), 5:17A and 5A:12 (a petition under § 8.01-675.5 must address each certification factor or may be denied). A separate order amends Rule 1A:1. A pending call for comment proposes requiring email service of pleadings in courts of record (new Rule 1:12A for courts not of record); it has not been adopted.","c":"Orders of Nov. 9, 2026 (eff. Jan. 8, 2027); Call for comment on electronic service of pleadings","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Rules of Supreme Court of Virginia, Parts One, Five and Five A (official compilation, vacourts.gov, PDF last modified September 15, 2026, including amendments effective September 15, 2026); Orders of November 9, 2026 amending Rules 5:17A and 5A:12 effective January 8, 2027; Code of Virginia §§ 8.01-626, 8.01-670, 8.01-670.2, 8.01-670.3, 8.01-671, 8.01-675.3, 8.01-675.5, 8.01-676.1, 17.1-405, 17.1-406, 17.1-408, 17.1-411 (law.lis.virginia.gov, displayed November 10, 2026).","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Virginia: notice of appeal to the Court of Appeals, then a petition to the Supreme Court","d":"Since January 1, 2022, a party aggrieved by any final circuit court judgment in a civil matter has an appeal of right to the Court of Appeals of Virginia, except in the categories that go directly to the Supreme Court (habeas corpus, collateral attacks on convictions, State Corporation Commission, and attorney-discipline proceedings). Review in the Supreme Court of Virginia is then by discretionary petition for appeal.\nThe appeal starts with a notice of appeal filed in the circuit court clerk’s office within 30 days after the judgment is signed. Post-trial motions do not stop that clock. Transcripts must be filed in the circuit court within 60 days. The record, not a joint appendix, is the default in most courts.\nAbout the reported “2025 expansion” of appeals of right: the collected text of § 17.1-405 shows the civil appeal of right coming from 2021 Sp. Sess. I, c. 489 (the repeal of former § 8.01-670.1 that came with it was effective January 1, 2022), with later amendments in 2022, 2023 and 2024 and no 2025 chapter in its history line. The 2025 changes found were rule amendments to Part Five A (effective July 1 and August 17, 2025). Treat any claimed 2025 statutory expansion as unconfirmed.","c":"Va. Code §§ 17.1-405, 17.1-406, 17.1-411, 8.01-675.3; Rules 5A:3, 5A:6, 5A:8"},"groups":[["Which court",[{"t":"Court of Appeals: appeals of right in civil cases","d":"Any aggrieved party may appeal to the Court of Appeals from any final judgment, order or decree of a circuit court in a civil matter; final circuit court decisions on appeal from an administrative agency or a § 2.2-3005 grievance decision; final Workers’ Compensation Commission decisions; and certain concealed-weapons-permit, prisoner-treatment and § 57-2.02 judgments.","c":"Va. Code § 17.1-405(A)(1)–(3), (6)"},{"t":"Direct to the Supreme Court","d":"Appeals go straight to the Supreme Court, and the Court of Appeals has no jurisdiction, in habeas corpus cases, actions collaterally attacking a criminal conviction (including § 8.01-428 motions), final State Corporation Commission orders, and proceedings under §§ 54.1-3935 and 54.1-3937. In an appeal direct from a trial court, the petition for appeal is due within 90 days after entry of the order.","c":"Va. Code §§ 17.1-406(B), 8.01-671(A); Rules 5:17(a)(1), 5:21"},{"t":"Partial final judgments in multi-party cases","d":"An order resolving claims as to fewer than all parties is appealable only if entered as a “Partial Final Judgment” with the findings Rule 1:2 requires; the time to appeal runs from that entry. Refusal to enter one is not appealable.","c":"Rule 1:2"}]],["Starting an appeal of right",[{"t":"Notice of appeal: 30 days, filed in the circuit court","d":"Within 30 days after entry of the final judgment or appealable order (the date the judge signs it), file a notice of appeal with the clerk of the trial court and send a copy to all opposing counsel. A copy also goes to the Court of Appeals clerk with the $50 filing fee; if the fee is not received within 10 days, the appeal is dismissed. The notice must say whether a transcript or written statement will be filed and must carry a certificate giving parties and counsel (with VSB numbers) and confirming that any transcript has been ordered. A notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed on and after entry.","c":"Rule 5A:6(a)–(d); Rule 5A:1(d)(11); Va. Code § 8.01-675.3"},{"t":"The deadline can be extended for good cause","d":"The notice-of-appeal deadline is mandatory, but the Court of Appeals may extend it on motion for good cause shown. Such a motion is timely if filed within 30 days after the deadline it seeks to extend. Filing the motion does not toll the deadline.","c":"Va. Code § 8.01-675.3; Rule 5A:3(a)–(c)"},{"t":"Appeal bond filed with the notice","d":"In an appeal of right in a civil case, the appellant must simultaneously file an appeal bond or irrevocable letter of credit of $500 (or the sum the trial court requires) for costs. To suspend execution, a suspending bond is required, capped at $50 million for an appellant and its affiliates combined (CPI adjustment from April 1, 2031) and including one year’s interest. The cost bond is not jurisdictional, and its deadline can be extended. The court may refuse suspension of support and custody decrees and of injunction rulings.","c":"Va. Code § 8.01-676.1(A), (C), (D), (J), (P); Rules 5A:6(a), 5A:16(a)"},{"t":"Post-trial motions do not toll","d":"The time for the notice of appeal is not extended by a motion for new trial, petition for rehearing or similar pleading. It restarts only if the trial court modifies, vacates or suspends the judgment within Rule 1:1’s 21 days, in which case it runs from the later final judgment. If the court vacates the judgment, an earlier notice of appeal becomes moot and a new one is required; if the judgment is only suspended or modified, the earlier notice stands.","c":"Rules 5A:3(a), 1:1(a), 1:1B(a)(2), 5A:6(a1)"},{"t":"Trial court jurisdiction after the notice","d":"The appellate court acquires jurisdiction immediately when the notice is filed. If the notice was filed within the 21-day window, the circuit court keeps plenary, concurrent jurisdiction until the window closes. After that, it keeps only limited jurisdiction, for matters such as stays, bond amount and form, and the other items listed in Rule 1:1B(a)(3).","c":"Rule 1:1B(a)(1)–(3)"}]],["Record and transcripts",[{"t":"Transcript within 60 days; notice of filing within 10 days","d":"A transcript is part of the record only if filed in the trial court clerk’s office within 60 days after entry of final judgment. A judge of the Court of Appeals may extend that only on a written motion filed within 90 days after entry, for good cause. Within 10 days after the transcript is filed (or after the notice of appeal, if the transcript came first), appellant’s counsel must give all counsel written notice of the filing date and file a copy with the trial court clerk. A failure that materially prejudices the appellee gets the transcript stricken.","c":"Rule 5A:8(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Electronic record is the norm","d":"Most circuit courts transmit an electronic record, and briefs cite record pages (“R. 1”). A joint appendix (“J.A.”) is used only where a paper record is filed. The appellant designates appendix contents within 15 days after the record is filed, and the appellee may add designations within 10 days.","c":"Rules 5A:10, 5A:10A, 5A:19(f), 5A:25"}]],["Briefing schedule (Court of Appeals)",[{"t":"Appeals of right","d":"Preliminary statement of assignments of error: 15 days after the record is filed in the Court of Appeals. Appellee’s preliminary assignments of cross-error: 10 days after that. Opening brief: 40 days after the record is filed. Appellee or guardian ad litem brief: 30 days after the opening brief, or 30 days after the record is filed, whichever is later. Reply: 14 days after the appellee’s brief. Reply in support of cross-error: 14 days after the appellant’s reply. Motions to extend must be filed within 10 days after the deadline expires.","c":"Rule 5A:19(b)"},{"t":"Discretionary appeals after a petition is granted","d":"Opening brief: 40 days after the clerk’s certificate of appeal. Appellee: 30 days after the opening brief. Reply: 14 days. Extension motions: within 10 days after the deadline.","c":"Rule 5A:19(c)"},{"t":"Cross-error","d":"In the Court of Appeals, an appellee raises its own claims of error as assignments of cross-error: preliminary cross-error within 10 days after the appellant’s preliminary statement, then in the appellee’s brief with record references showing preservation. The collected rules do not separately say whether an appellee seeking affirmative relief must also file its own notice of appeal; if in doubt, file one within 30 days.","c":"Rules 5A:19(b)(1), (5), 5A:21(b), (e), 5A:22"}]],["Length, format, and content",[{"t":"Word and page limits (Court of Appeals)","d":"Opening and appellee briefs: the longer of 50 pages or 12,300 words. Reply: the longer of 20 pages or 3,500 words. Petition for appeal and brief in opposition: the longer of 35 pages or 7,500 words. Petition for rehearing (panel or en banc): the longer of 25 pages or 5,300 words. Headings, footnotes and quotations count. The cover, tables, signature blocks and certificate do not. The certificate of service must state the page or word count.","c":"Rules 5A:19(a), 5A:12(d), 5A:13(b)(1), 5A:33(a), 5A:34(a), 5A:1(c)(4)"},{"t":"Word and page limits (Supreme Court)","d":"Petition for appeal: the longer of 35 pages or 6,125 words. Brief in opposition: the longer of 25 pages or 4,375 words. Merits briefs: the longer of 50 pages or 8,750 words. Reply: the longer of 15 pages or 2,625 words. Petition for review under § 8.01-626: the longer of 20 pages or 3,500 words. Petition for rehearing: the longer of 10 pages or 1,750 words.","c":"Rules 5:17(f), 5:18(b), 5:26(b), 5:17A(c)(i), 5:37(d)"},{"t":"Assignments of error are jurisdiction-like","d":"Each assignment of error must identify the specific ruling (or failure to rule) and give an exact reference to where it was preserved. An assignment that says only that the judgment is contrary to the law and the evidence is insufficient. A petition with no assignments of error is dismissed. Arguments may not be incorporated by reference to filings below. Unpublished dispositions may be cited only as informative.","c":"Rules 5A:12(c)(1), 5A:19(e), 5A:20, 5A:1(f), 5:17(c)(1)"},{"t":"Preservation","d":"A ruling is a basis for reversal only if an objection was stated with reasonable certainty at the time of the ruling, unless good cause is shown or review is needed to attain the ends of justice.","c":"Rules 5A:18, 5:25"}]],["Interlocutory appeals",[{"t":"Appeals of right from certain interlocutory orders","d":"An interlocutory decree or order involving an equitable claim that requires money to be paid, requires possession or title of property to change, or adjudicates the principles of a cause may be appealed to the Court of Appeals. The notice of appeal is due within 30 days of the order. This does not apply to listed domestic-relations and non-final protective orders.","c":"Va. Code §§ 17.1-405(A)(5), (B), 8.01-675.3"},{"t":"Certified questions by permission (§ 8.01-675.5)","d":"Before trial, a party may ask the circuit court to certify an otherwise non-appealable order. The court must find a question of law with substantial ground for difference of opinion, no clear controlling Virginia appellate precedent, a determination that will dispose of a material aspect of the case, and that appeal is in the parties’ best interest. The petition for appeal is due in the Court of Appeals within 15 days after the certification order, and no notice of appeal is needed. A brief in opposition is due 7 days after service. Proceedings are not stayed unless ordered. Failing to seek review does not waive the issue. From January 8, 2027, the petition must address each of the four factors or may be denied.","c":"Va. Code § 8.01-675.5; Rules 5A:6(a2), 5A:12(a)(2), (c)(5A) (eff. Jan. 8, 2027), 5A:13(a)(2)"},{"t":"15-day petitions for review in the Supreme Court","d":"A petition for review may be filed with the Supreme Court within 15 days after a circuit court order that grants, refuses, dissolves or refuses to enlarge a preliminary injunction; grants or denies a pretrial plea of sovereign, absolute or qualified immunity; or (since 2026) grants, denies or reviews an order closing a proceeding. A three-Justice panel decides. The response is due 15 days after service. The petition must attach the pertinent record. The deadline is mandatory, with a single extension of up to 30 days if two Justices concur.","c":"Va. Code §§ 8.01-626, 8.01-670.2, 8.01-670.3; Rules 5:17A, 5:5(a), 1:1C"},{"t":"Mass-tort (MCLA) orders","d":"Petitions under § 8.01-267.8 are due 10 days after the trial court’s order, and the brief in opposition 7 days after service.","c":"Rules 5A:12(a)(1), 5A:13(a)(1)"}]],["Oral argument and decision",[{"t":"Argument and summary disposition","d":"In appeals of right and granted appeals, argument is allowed unless the parties agree it is unnecessary or the panel unanimously finds the appeal wholly without merit, controlled by settled authority, or adequately presented on the papers. The clerk gives at least 15 days’ notice, and each side gets 15 minutes. Amici argue only with leave.","c":"Rules 5A:27, 5A:28"},{"t":"Amicus briefs","d":"The United States and the Commonwealth may file without leave; anyone else needs leave by motion. A brief is timely if filed no later than 7 days after the principal brief of the party supported.","c":"Rule 5A:23"}]],["Rehearing",[{"t":"Court of Appeals: panel rehearing and rehearing en banc","d":"A petition for panel rehearing, or for rehearing en banc, of a decision finally disposing of a case is due within 14 days. No response is received unless requested, and there is no oral argument on the petition. The court may order en banc rehearing on its own motion within 20 days. If en banc rehearing is granted, the mandate is stayed as to the panel’s issues. Since July 1, 2026, review is limited to the issues granted, and an en banc court of fewer than all judges has 17 members.","c":"Rules 5A:33, 5A:34, 5A:35 (as amended eff. July 1, 2026)"},{"t":"Denial of a petition for appeal","d":"In the Court of Appeals, a petition for rehearing of a denied petition for appeal is due within 14 days. In the Supreme Court, rehearing of a refused or dismissed petition for appeal is due within 14 days.","c":"Rules 5A:15, 5:20(b)(1)"},{"t":"Supreme Court rehearing after a merits decision","d":"File a written notice of intent within 10 days after the decision (this holds the mandate), then the petition within 30 days. The limit is the longer of 10 pages or 1,750 words.","c":"Rule 5:37(b)–(d)"}]],["Review in the Supreme Court of Virginia",[{"t":"Notice of appeal plus petition for appeal, both within 30 days","d":"To seek review of a Court of Appeals decision, file a notice of appeal with the clerk of the Court of Appeals within 30 days after entry of the final judgment or of the order denying a timely rehearing petition. Separately file the petition for appeal in the Supreme Court within 30 days after the judgment or rehearing denial. The petition deadline may be extended for good cause. Only assignments of error relating to issues presented to, and actions taken by, the Court of Appeals may be raised.","c":"Rules 5:14(a), 5:17(a)(2), (c)(1)(ii); Va. Code §§ 8.01-671(B), 17.1-411"},{"t":"Brief in opposition and merits briefing","d":"The brief in opposition is due 21 days after service of the petition. If the petition is granted, the opening brief and appendix are due 40 days after the certificate of appeal, the appellee’s brief 25 days after the opening brief, and the reply 14 days after that. Granting is discretionary.","c":"Rules 5:18(a), 5:26(c), 5:23; Va. Code § 17.1-411"},{"t":"Deadlines are strict","d":"The times for a notice of appeal, a § 8.01-626 petition for review, and rehearing petitions in the Supreme Court are mandatory. A single extension of up to 30 days may be granted only if at least two Justices concur on a good-cause motion.","c":"Rule 5:5(a) (as amended eff. Sept. 15, 2026)"}]],["Filing, fees, and service",[{"t":"Mandatory e-filing through VACES","d":"In both appellate courts, everything other than the record must be filed electronically in PDF through the Virginia Appellate Courts Electronic System and served electronically. Pro se litigants (Court of Appeals) and pro se prisoners or others with leave may file on paper, and paper filers may use priority, express, registered or certified mail or next-day commercial carrier to be timely. Each filing needs a certificate of service stating the date and manner of service and, where limits apply, the word or page count.","c":"Rules 5:1B, 5:5(c), 5A:1(c), 5A:3(d)"},{"t":"Fees and out-of-state counsel","d":"The Court of Appeals filing fee is $50, due with the notice of appeal or petition for appeal. Out-of-state lawyers must be admitted under Rule 1A:4 ($250 per case, with Virginia local counsel) to argue.","c":"Rules 5A:6(c), 5A:12(e), 5A:28(c), 1A:4"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal to the Court of Appeals (final judgment or appealable interlocutory order)","d":"30 days from entry (judge’s signature) of the judgment or order.\nFiled with the circuit court clerk. Extendable by the Court of Appeals for good cause; the motion must be filed within 30 days after the deadline (Rule 5A:3(a), (c)).","c":"Va. Code § 8.01-675.3; Rule 5A:6(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry)","note":"Filed with the circuit court clerk. Extendable by the Court of Appeals for good cause; the motion must be filed within 30 days after the deadline (Rule 5A:3(a), (c))."}},{"t":"Trial court’s control over final judgment","d":"21 days from entry of final judgment.\nPost-trial motions do not extend the appeal time unless the judgment is modified, vacated or suspended within this period (Rule 5A:3(a)).","c":"Rule 1:1(a)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (entry of final judgment)","note":"Post-trial motions do not extend the appeal time unless the judgment is modified, vacated or suspended within this period (Rule 5A:3(a))."}},{"t":"Appeal bond (appeal of right, civil)","d":"0 days from filed simultaneously with the notice of appeal.\n$500 or the sum the trial court sets. Not jurisdictional; extendable.","c":"Va. Code § 8.01-676.1(A), (P); Rule 5A:6(a)","due":{"n":0,"from":"the trigger date (filed simultaneously with the notice of appeal)","note":"$500 or the sum the trial court sets. Not jurisdictional; extendable."}},{"t":"Court of Appeals filing fee","d":"10 days from filing of the notice of appeal (grace period).\n$50; the appeal is dismissed if the fee is not received within 10 days.","c":"Rule 5A:6(c)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"$50; the appeal is dismissed if the fee is not received within 10 days."}},{"t":"Transcript filed in circuit court","d":"60 days from entry of final judgment.\nExtension only on a written motion filed within 90 days after entry.","c":"Rule 5A:8(a)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (entry of final judgment)","note":"Extension only on a written motion filed within 90 days after entry."}},{"t":"Notice of filing transcript","d":"10 days from filing of the transcript (or of the notice of appeal, if later).","c":"Rule 5A:8(b)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the transcript)","note":null}},{"t":"Preliminary statement of assignments of error","d":"15 days from filing of the record in the Court of Appeals.\nAppellee’s preliminary cross-error is due 10 days after the appellant’s statement.","c":"Rule 5A:19(b)(1)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the record in the Court of Appeals)","note":"Appellee’s preliminary cross-error is due 10 days after the appellant’s statement."}},{"t":"Opening brief (appeal of right)","d":"40 days from filing of the record in the Court of Appeals.\nLonger of 50 pages or 12,300 words.","c":"Rule 5A:19(b)(2)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the record in the Court of Appeals)","note":"Longer of 50 pages or 12,300 words."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief (Court of Appeals)","d":"30 days from filing of the opening brief (or of the record, if later).","c":"Rule 5A:19(b)(3)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the opening brief)","note":null}},{"t":"Reply brief (Court of Appeals)","d":"14 days from filing of the appellee’s brief.\nLonger of 20 pages or 3,500 words.","c":"Rule 5A:19(b)(4)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the appellee’s brief)","note":"Longer of 20 pages or 3,500 words."}},{"t":"Motion to extend a Court of Appeals briefing deadline","d":"10 days from expiration of the deadline.","c":"Rule 5A:19(b)(6), (c)(4)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the deadline)","note":null}},{"t":"Petition for appeal of a certified interlocutory order (§ 8.01-675.5)","d":"15 days from entry of the circuit court’s certification.\nBrief in opposition due 7 days after service (Rule 5A:13(a)(2)).","c":"Va. Code § 8.01-675.5(A); Rule 5A:12(a)(2)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the circuit court’s certification)","note":"Brief in opposition due 7 days after service (Rule 5A:13(a)(2))."}},{"t":"Petition for review in the Supreme Court (injunction, immunity, or closure order)","d":"15 days from entry of the circuit court order.\nResponse due 15 days after service. One extension of up to 30 days if two Justices concur (Rule 5:5(a)).","c":"Va. Code §§ 8.01-626, 8.01-670.2, 8.01-670.3; Rule 5:17A(a)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the circuit court order)","note":"Response due 15 days after service. One extension of up to 30 days if two Justices concur (Rule 5:5(a))."}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing or rehearing en banc (Court of Appeals)","d":"14 days from the decision finally disposing of the case.\nExtendable for good cause (Rule 5A:3(a)). The court may order en banc rehearing on its own motion within 20 days.","c":"Rules 5A:33(a), 5A:34(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (the decision finally disposing of the case)","note":"Extendable for good cause (Rule 5A:3(a)). The court may order en banc rehearing on its own motion within 20 days."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal to the Supreme Court (from the Court of Appeals)","d":"30 days from entry of the Court of Appeals judgment or of the order denying timely rehearing.\nFiled with the clerk of the Court of Appeals. Mandatory (Rule 5:5(a)).","c":"Rule 5:14(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the Court of Appeals judgment or of the order denying timely rehearing)","note":"Filed with the clerk of the Court of Appeals. Mandatory (Rule 5:5(a))."}},{"t":"Petition for appeal to the Supreme Court (from the Court of Appeals)","d":"30 days from entry of the judgment or of the denial of timely rehearing.\nExtension possible for good cause.","c":"Va. Code § 8.01-671(B); Rule 5:17(a)(2)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or of the denial of timely rehearing)","note":"Extension possible for good cause."}},{"t":"Petition for appeal direct from a trial court to the Supreme Court","d":"90 days from entry of the order appealed from.\nOnly in categories that go directly to the Supreme Court (e.g., habeas, § 8.01-428 collateral attacks).","c":"Va. Code § 8.01-671(A); Rule 5:17(a)(1)","due":{"n":90,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order appealed from)","note":"Only in categories that go directly to the Supreme Court (e.g., habeas, § 8.01-428 collateral attacks)."}},{"t":"Brief in opposition to a petition for appeal (Supreme Court)","d":"21 days from service of the petition.","c":"Rule 5:18(a)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (service of the petition)","note":null}},{"t":"Opening brief after a granted petition (Supreme Court)","d":"40 days from date of the certificate of appeal.\nAppellee’s brief 25 days later; reply 14 days after that (Rule 5:26(c)(2)–(3)).","c":"Rule 5:26(c)(1)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (date of the certificate of appeal)","note":"Appellee’s brief 25 days later; reply 14 days after that (Rule 5:26(c)(2)–(3))."}},{"t":"Notice of intent to seek rehearing (Supreme Court)","d":"10 days from the Supreme Court’s decision.\nThe petition itself is due within 30 days after the decision (Rule 5:37(d)).","c":"Rule 5:37(b)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (the Supreme Court’s decision)","note":"The petition itself is due within 30 days after the decision (Rule 5:37(d))."}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing after refusal of a petition for appeal (Supreme Court)","d":"14 days from date of the order refusing or dismissing the petition.","c":"Rule 5:20(b)(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (date of the order refusing or dismissing the petition)","note":null}},{"t":"Increase in appeal or suspending bond ordered by a court","d":"15 days from the order requiring the increase.\nIf not effected, the appeal is dismissed (cost bond) or suspension ends (suspending bond).","c":"Va. Code § 8.01-676.1(E)(3)–(4)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (the order requiring the increase)","note":"If not effected, the appeal is dismissed (cost bond) or suspension ends (suspending bond)."}}]]],"dl":[["Notice of appeal to the Court of Appeals (final judgment or appealable interlocutory order)",30,"Entry (judge’s signature) of the judgment or order","Va. Code § 8.01-675.3; Rule 5A:6(a)","Filed with the circuit court clerk. Extendable by the Court of Appeals for good cause; the motion must be filed within 30 days after the deadline (Rule 5A:3(a), (c))."],["Trial court’s control over final judgment",21,"Entry of final judgment","Rule 1:1(a)","Post-trial motions do not extend the appeal time unless the judgment is modified, vacated or suspended within this period (Rule 5A:3(a))."],["Appeal bond (appeal of right, civil)",0,"Filed simultaneously with the notice of appeal","Va. Code § 8.01-676.1(A), (P); Rule 5A:6(a)","$500 or the sum the trial court sets. Not jurisdictional; extendable."],["Court of Appeals filing fee",10,"Filing of the notice of appeal (grace period)","Rule 5A:6(c)","$50; the appeal is dismissed if the fee is not received within 10 days."],["Transcript filed in circuit court",60,"Entry of final judgment","Rule 5A:8(a)","Extension only on a written motion filed within 90 days after entry."],["Notice of filing transcript",10,"Filing of the transcript (or of the notice of appeal, if later)","Rule 5A:8(b)",""],["Preliminary statement of assignments of error",15,"Filing of the record in the Court of Appeals","Rule 5A:19(b)(1)","Appellee’s preliminary cross-error is due 10 days after the appellant’s statement."],["Opening brief (appeal of right)",40,"Filing of the record in the Court of Appeals","Rule 5A:19(b)(2)","Longer of 50 pages or 12,300 words."],["Appellee’s brief (Court of Appeals)",30,"Filing of the opening brief (or of the record, if later)","Rule 5A:19(b)(3)",""],["Reply brief (Court of Appeals)",14,"Filing of the appellee’s brief","Rule 5A:19(b)(4)","Longer of 20 pages or 3,500 words."],["Motion to extend a Court of Appeals briefing deadline",10,"Expiration of the deadline","Rule 5A:19(b)(6), (c)(4)",""],["Petition for appeal of a certified interlocutory order (§ 8.01-675.5)",15,"Entry of the circuit court’s certification","Va. Code § 8.01-675.5(A); Rule 5A:12(a)(2)","Brief in opposition due 7 days after service (Rule 5A:13(a)(2))."],["Petition for review in the Supreme Court (injunction, immunity, or closure order)",15,"Entry of the circuit court order","Va. Code §§ 8.01-626, 8.01-670.2, 8.01-670.3; Rule 5:17A(a)","Response due 15 days after service. One extension of up to 30 days if two Justices concur (Rule 5:5(a))."],["Petition for rehearing or rehearing en banc (Court of Appeals)",14,"The decision finally disposing of the case","Rules 5A:33(a), 5A:34(a)","Extendable for good cause (Rule 5A:3(a)). The court may order en banc rehearing on its own motion within 20 days."],["Notice of appeal to the Supreme Court (from the Court of Appeals)",30,"Entry of the Court of Appeals judgment or of the order denying timely rehearing","Rule 5:14(a)","Filed with the clerk of the Court of Appeals. Mandatory (Rule 5:5(a))."],["Petition for appeal to the Supreme Court (from the Court of Appeals)",30,"Entry of the judgment or of the denial of timely rehearing","Va. Code § 8.01-671(B); Rule 5:17(a)(2)","Extension possible for good cause."],["Petition for appeal direct from a trial court to the Supreme Court",90,"Entry of the order appealed from","Va. Code § 8.01-671(A); Rule 5:17(a)(1)","Only in categories that go directly to the Supreme Court (e.g., habeas, § 8.01-428 collateral attacks)."],["Brief in opposition to a petition for appeal (Supreme Court)",21,"Service of the petition","Rule 5:18(a)",""],["Opening brief after a granted petition (Supreme Court)",40,"Date of the certificate of appeal","Rule 5:26(c)(1)","Appellee’s brief 25 days later; reply 14 days after that (Rule 5:26(c)(2)–(3))."],["Notice of intent to seek rehearing (Supreme Court)",10,"The Supreme Court’s decision","Rule 5:37(b)","The petition itself is due within 30 days after the decision (Rule 5:37(d))."],["Petition for rehearing after refusal of a petition for appeal (Supreme Court)",14,"Date of the order refusing or dismissing the petition","Rule 5:20(b)(1)",""],["Increase in appeal or suspending bond ordered by a court",15,"The order requiring the increase","Va. Code § 8.01-676.1(E)(3)–(4)","If not effected, the appeal is dismissed (cost bond) or suspension ends (suspending bond)."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.vacourts.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Rules of Supreme Court of Virginia","rulesSrc":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"NOTE","t":"What this file covers","s":"Virginia civil procedure in the circuit courts lives in Parts One (rules for all proceedings), Three (civil actions) and Four (discovery and pretrial) of the Rules of Supreme Court of Virginia, plus Title 8.01 of the Code of Virginia (Civil Remedies and Procedure), which is statutory and is not summarized rule by rule here. Key statutes to read alongside the rules: Code § 8.01-271.1 (signing and sanctions; amended 2026, defective signatures are voidable and must be cured within 21 days), § 8.01-275.1 (service within 12 months of filing is timely; later service needs a due-diligence finding), § 8.01-380 (one nonsuit as of right before a motion to strike is sustained or the case is submitted; possible expert-cost award if notice comes within 7 days of trial) and § 8.01-229(E) (tolling; refiling within 6 months after a voluntary nonsuit). Rules text is from the official compilation PDF last modified September 15, 2026 (including amendments effective that date). An Order of November 9, 2026 amends Rules 4:12 and 4:15 effective January 8, 2027; those changes are not yet in effect and are flagged in those rules’ summaries. Part One’s appendix forms (praecipe, jury instructions, Uniform Pretrial Scheduling Order) are described under Rules 1:15 and 1:18; Part Three’s appendix of forms is not summarized.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:1","t":"Finality of Judgments, Orders and Decrees","s":"A final judgment stays under the trial court’s control, and can be modified, vacated or suspended, for 21 days after entry and no longer; the entry date is the date the judge signs it (on paper or electronically). A judgment is final if it disposes of all claims against all parties and leaves only ministerial execution; an order sustaining a demurrer or plea in bar, or granting summary judgment, disposes of the claim even without express dismissal (and a demurrer sustained with leave to amend becomes dispositive if the amendment is not filed in time), but an order that merely grants a motion to strike the evidence does not. The text makes no exception for pending post-judgment motions, so only an order modifying, vacating or suspending the judgment within the 21 days keeps it under the court’s control (compare the 28-day federal post-judgment motion periods).","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:1A","t":"Recovery of Appellate Attorney Fees in Circuit Court","s":"A prevailing appellee who recovered attorney fees or costs in circuit court under a contract, statute or other law may apply to that circuit court for fees and costs incurred on appeal by filing the application and a copy of the final appellate judgment within 30 days after the final appellate judgment (the mandate, or the final appellate order if no mandate issues; for a petition under Rule 5:17, the later of the denial of the petition or of rehearing). The case is reinstated on the docket, the court keeps jurisdiction to decide the application, and its ruling is a final order under Rule 1:1; other remedies are not displaced.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:1B","t":"Jurisdictional Transfer During Appeal of Final or Partial Final Judgment in Circuit Court","s":"Filing a notice of appeal gives the appellate court jurisdiction, but if it is filed within Rule 1:1’s 21 days the circuit court keeps full concurrent jurisdiction until day 21 (a vacatur in that window moots the notice, and a new notice is needed from any later final judgment). After day 21 the circuit court keeps only limited jurisdiction, for example over stays, appeal and suspending bonds under Code § 8.01-676.1, enforcement and contempt, clerical corrections (with appellate leave once an appeal is pending), appointment of counsel, and acts other statutes or rules allow after 21 days. Any party may move in the appellate court to dismiss a moot appeal after day 21, and the appellate court may temporarily remand for fact-finding.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:1C","t":"Jurisdictional Transfer During Appeal of Interlocutory Orders","s":"When an interlocutory order is appealed under Code §§ 8.01-626, 8.01-670.2, 8.01-670.3, 17.1-405(A)(4) or 17.1-405(A)(5), the appellate court has exclusive jurisdiction over that order while the circuit court keeps the rest of the case unless either court stays proceedings. On motion for good cause, the appellate court may let the circuit court withdraw or modify the order (last amended effective September 15, 2026).","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:2","t":"Appeal From Partial Final Judgment in Multi-Party Cases","s":"In a multi-party civil action the court may enter final judgment as to fewer than all parties only by an order expressly labeled “Partial Final Judgment” with findings that the claims against those parties are separate and distinct and that the appeal and the remaining claims cannot affect each other. Entry of that order starts the time to note an appeal under Rule 5A:6; refusal to enter one is not appealable, and any other order resolving fewer than all claims or parties is not final.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:3","t":"Reporters and Transcripts of Proceedings in Courts","s":"Court reporters must be sworn and are subject to the judge’s control. Any interested person may obtain a transcript on terms the judge sets, and proceedings may be recorded by any device the judge approves.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:4","t":"General Provisions as to Pleadings","s":"Counsel’s tender of a pleading certifies good faith and no purpose of delay; pleadings must be signed with an address, state facts in numbered paragraphs, and need only clearly inform the other side of the true nature of the claim or defense (brevity is “enjoined”). Allegations not denied when a responsive pleading is required are deemed admitted, denials must fairly meet the substance, and pleading lack of knowledge counts as a denial; exhibits mentioned in a pleading become part of it. Alternative and inconsistent claims are allowed if they arise from the same transaction or occurrence, and every paper must show counsel’s Virginia State Bar number, office address, phone, and any regularly used email and fax.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:5","t":"Counsel and Parties Appearing Without Counsel","s":"A firm signature must include at least one individual lawyer’s signature (electronic or digital-image signatures count for Code § 8.01-271.1), service on one firm member is service on the firm, and “counsel of record” includes anyone who signed a pleading, gave written notice of appearance, or endorsed a draft order; self-represented parties must sign every paper, state an address, and file and update a residence and mailing address. Counsel may withdraw only by leave of court after notice to the client (the order must name substitute counsel or give the client’s contact information) or by completing a limited scope appearance. Limited scope appearances are allowed by notice for lawyers with or referred by a qualified legal services provider, or by leave of court; papers must be served on both lawyer and client, and if the client will not endorse a notice of completion the lawyer may move to terminate with seven days for objection; a single district court hearing appearance ends automatically.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:5A","t":"Signature Defects","s":"A paper not signed as Code § 8.01-271.1(A) requires is defective and voidable; the defect must be raised in the trial court before its jurisdiction ends under Rules 1:1 and 1:1B (or in the appellate court for appellate filings) or it is waived. The defect must be cured within 21 days after it is brought to the filer’s attention, in which case the paper relates back; otherwise it is stricken, and the court may award fees and costs caused by the defect. If a timely complaint is dismissed for failure to cure, Code § 8.01-229(E)(1) tolls the limitation period while it was pending.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:6","t":"Res Judicata Claim Preclusion","s":"A final judgment on the merits bars any later civil action against the same parties (including those in privity) on any claim arising from the same conduct, transaction or occurrence, whatever the legal theory, elements, evidence or remedy, and this covers claims in complaints, counterclaims, cross-claims and third-party pleadings. It applies to judgments in civil actions commenced after July 1, 2006, and does not bar separate personal injury and property damage suits or later claims after mechanic’s lien proceedings to the extent previously allowed.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:7","t":"Computation of Response Dates","s":"When a deadline runs from service of a paper on counsel, no days are added for hand delivery, same-day courier, fax or email completed by 5:00 p.m.; one day is added for next-day commercial delivery or for hand delivery, fax or email completed after 5:00 p.m. but before midnight; and three days are added for service by mail. In Parts Five and Five A this applies only to the time to file a brief in opposition.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:8","t":"Amendments","s":"No pleading may be amended after filing except by leave of court, which should be liberally granted; a written motion for leave must attach the proposed amended pleading, which is deemed filed on the date of the order granting leave. Where leave is granted other than on a written motion (for example, on sustaining a demurrer), the amended pleading must be filed within 21 days after leave is granted unless the court sets another time. Unlike Federal Rule 15(a)(1), the text gives no amendment as of right.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:9","t":"Discretion of Court","s":"The court may review and correct all clerk’s-office steps in maturing a case, and may extend the time to file pleadings even after it has expired. The time to file a motion objecting to venue may never be extended except as Code § 8.01-264 permits.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:10","t":"Verification","s":"If a statute requires a pleading to be sworn or supported by affidavit and it is not, but it contains the required allegations, an objection must be made by motion to strike within seven days after the pleading is filed or it is waived. The pleading may be sworn or the affidavit filed before the court rules on the motion or within any time the court allows.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:11","t":"Motion to Strike the Evidence","s":"If the court sustains a motion to strike the evidence in a jury trial, it should enter summary judgment or partial summary judgment consistent with that ruling. If the motion was overruled and the jury hangs, the movant may renew it immediately after the jury is discharged, and the court should enter judgment if it concludes it erred.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:12","t":"Service of Papers after the Initial Process","s":"Papers after initial process (and requests for subpoenas duces tecum) must be served on each counsel of record on or before the day of filing by hand delivery, same-day or next-day commercial delivery, fax, mail, or email when Rule 1:17 applies or the person served has consented in writing; service is effective on delivery, dispatch, transmission or mailing, but email service fails if the sender learns it did not arrive. Each paper must carry an acceptance of service or a certificate of service stating the date and method, and when service is by email the certificate must also be mailed or faxed to each counsel on or before the day of service.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:13","t":"Endorsements","s":"Draft orders and decrees must be endorsed by counsel of record, or reasonable notice of the time and place of presentation, with copies, must be served under Rule 1:12 on counsel who have not endorsed them. The court may modify or dispense with this, and in electronically filed cases endorsements and objections may be handled under Rule 1:17.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:14","t":"Preservation of the Record","s":"A court may authorize electronic or photographic means to preserve the record or parts of it.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:15","t":"Local Rules of Court","s":"A circuit court’s local rules must be entered in the order book, posted in the clerk’s office, filed with the Executive Secretary and furnished to regular practitioners; a lawyer appearing in an unfamiliar circuit is responsible for learning and following its local rules. Local praecipe and pre-trial jury-instruction rules must substantially follow the Part One appendix forms (the instruction form requires proposed instructions with authorities two days before a civil jury trial), and chief circuit and juvenile court judges must send court-management information to the Executive Secretary by December 31 each year.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:16","t":"Filing Format and Procedure","s":"Except in electronically filed cases, filed papers must be on 8 1/2 by 11 inch pages and typed material double spaced except quotations (oversized tables, charts, plats and photos excepted), and transcripts must be filed one transcript page per page, never condensed. The clerk may not refuse a noncompliant paper but may require a corrected substitute certified as identical in content.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:17","t":"Electronic Filing and Service","s":"Governs courts with electronic filing systems: filings in an Electronically Filed Case must be electronic PDF documents unless otherwise provided, are deemed filed when received by the e-filing portal at any hour (but receipt on a weekend, holiday or closed day counts as filed the next open day), and portal outages during the last filing hours of a business day are treated as a clerk’s-office closing for computing deadlines if the filer gives the clerk proof of the attempt on the next business day. Unless the parties agree or the court orders otherwise, post-process service in such cases must be electronic, the approved electronic identification is the filer’s signature, counsel’s email must appear on filings, and hyperlinks are allowed only within the filing or to other filings in the same case. The rule also sets system security standards, clerk notices for fee or wrong-case defects, and electronic circulation and endorsement of draft orders.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:18","t":"Pretrial Scheduling Order","s":"Parties may submit an agreed pretrial scheduling order, and if none is otherwise entered the court may (on request or on its own) enter the Uniform Pretrial Scheduling Order in the Part One appendix; except in domestic relations and eminent domain cases, the court may deviate from it only by agreement of all counsel or after a hearing and a finding that it does not fit the case. The uniform order requires discovery to be complete 30 days before trial (trial depositions until 15 days before), expert identification 90 days before trial for claimants, 60 days for defendants and 45 days for rebuttal, exchange and filing of exhibit and witness lists 15 days before trial with objections 5 days before, non-party deposition designations 30 days before trial with objections within 7 days and hearing at least 5 days before trial, proposed jury instructions exchanged two business days before trial, and motions in limine needing more than five minutes heard before trial day. The alternate eminent domain order uses 120/90/60-day expert deadlines and requires at least six commissioner nominations per party when a commission is used.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:19","t":"Pretrial Conferences","s":"In addition to Rule 4:13 conferences, the court may schedule a final pretrial conference on request or on its own, and must do so in cases set for five or more trial days if any counsel asks at least 45 days before trial. The conference may be held in person, by phone or video and may address settlement, issues for trial, stipulations, limits on witnesses, pending motions in limine and jury instructions.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:20","t":"Scheduling Civil Cases for Trial","s":"Each circuit court must use one or more listed methods to set civil trials: counsel’s agreed date approved by phone or electronic communication, a Rule 1:18 written plan, a scheduling conference, or a docket call under Code § 17.1-517 (with or without a praecipe). The procedures must be available online through the Executive Secretary and in each clerk’s office.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:21","t":"Preliminary Voir Dire Information","s":"At the start of jury selection in any civil or criminal case the court must explain the purpose of voir dire and the difference between peremptory and cause challenges, summarize the case, estimate trial length, and say whether sequestration is expected.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:22","t":"Exercise of Challenges to Prospective Jurors","s":"Counsel must be given the opportunity to challenge jurors for cause outside the presence of the panel.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:23","t":"Note Taking by Jurors","s":"The court may let jurors take notes; if so, it must collect and secure them at the end of each trial day and collect and destroy them when the trial ends.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:24","t":"Requirements for Court Payment Agreements for the Collection of Fines and Costs","s":"Criminal and traffic-side rule: any defendant may enter a deferred, modified deferred or installment agreement to pay fines and costs, with installments of $25 a month or more based on ability to pay (or less if indigent), no required down payment except on a re-entry after default (capped at 10 percent if $500 or less owed, otherwise the greater of 5 percent or $50). Payments within 10 days of the due date are timely, modifications may be requested at any time, community service credit may be offered (not against restitution), and a defendant whose sole resource is Social Security or SSI is exempt from payment and may not be sent to collections.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:25","t":"Specialty Dockets","s":"Criminal-side rule: specialty dockets are judicially monitored treatment dockets, and only three types are recognized (recovery court, veterans and behavioral health dockets); grouping cases by area of law is not a specialty docket. A circuit or district court must get Supreme Court authorization to start or continue one, new types need Supreme Court recognition, and advisory committees set operating standards while localities bear the funding.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:26","t":"File Format Requirements for Pleadings, Motions, Notices, Briefs and Exhibits Sought to be Filed or Admitted in Electronic or Digital Form","s":"Electronic documents made part of a court record are public unless sealed or otherwise provided by law. The filer or offeror must supply them in an unencrypted, non-proprietary, open-source format or provide the software needed to view them.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"1:27","t":"Testimony by Audiovisual Means in Circuit Court Civil Cases","s":"The court may allow live testimony by audiovisual means in any civil trial or hearing on a motion made at least 15 days before the trial or hearing (unless the court allows shorter notice), with written objections due 5 days after service of a written motion unless the court sets otherwise. The offering party must supply and pay for equipment and arrangements unless the courtroom meets Code § 19.2-3.1 standards, and failure to set up properly bars the testimony; remote testimony is presumptively allowed on consent of all parties or for lay witnesses more than 100 miles away or out of state, treating health professionals, certain hospital superintendents more than 30 miles away, and public officials whose duties prevent attendance.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:1","t":"Scope","s":"There is one form of action, the civil action, covering legal and equitable claims in circuit court, including cases appealed or removed from lower courts where applicable. The rules do not apply to habeas corpus petitions, and established practice continues for matters the rules do not cover.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:2","t":"Commencement of Civil Actions","s":"A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint (or a “Petition” where statute or practice requires) in the clerk’s office, and the writ tax and clerk’s fees must be paid before a summons issues. The caption must name the court and all parties, the complaint need only ask for the specific relief sought, and every complaint seeking money damages must include an ad damnum clause stating the amount sought (amendable under Rule 1:8).","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:3","t":"Filing of Pleadings; Return of Certain Writs","s":"The clerk must file all pleadings when tendered and note the filing date; disputes over a right to file go to the court. A case may be designated an Electronically Filed Case only with consent of all parties, and certain originals (sworn pleadings, wills, contracts and deeds, settlement agreements, negotiable instruments, and others) must be flagged for retention. No writ may be returnable more than 90 days after its date unless a statute allows longer, and the clerk must issue additional summonses on request.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:4","t":"Copies of Complaint","s":"Unless service is waived under Code § 8.01-286.1, the plaintiff must give the clerk one paper copy of the complaint for each defendant to be served (also in electronically filed cases); physical copies of exhibits need not be served, but an electronic copy of exhibits incorporated by reference must be filed with the complaint. A shortage of copies does not affect the action’s pendency, but if copies are not furnished by a date the court sets, the court may dismiss.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:5","t":"The Summons","s":"The summons tells the defendant that unless a written responsive pleading is filed within 21 days after service, the allegations may be taken as admitted and judgment entered by default or after evidence. Defendants may appear voluntarily or waive process (in divorce or annulment only as statutes allow), and summonses need not issue for persons under a disability except in divorce, annulment or in personam cases. No judgment may be entered against a defendant served more than one year after the action was filed against that defendant unless the court finds the plaintiff used due diligence to serve timely (see also Code § 8.01-275.1; this one-year window is much longer than the federal 90-day service period).","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:6","t":"Proof of Service","s":"Returns are made on a “Proof of Service” form, and the summons and attached complaint are served as one paper. Process servers must serve within five days after receipt and make return within 72 hours after the earliest service shown on each Proof of Service, but late service or return does not prejudice any party except as Rule 3:5 provides.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:7","t":"Bills of Particulars","s":"On a prompt motion the court may order a bill of particulars to amplify a pleading that does not give fair notice, and may strike an inadequate bill (and ultimately the underlying pleading) on a prompt motion; the order must fix the filing time. A defendant must respond to a complaint amplified by a bill of particulars within 21 days after it is filed unless relying on pleadings already filed, and responses to other amplified pleadings are due within 21 days or as the court sets.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:8","t":"Answers, Pleas, Demurrers and Motions","s":"A defendant must file responsive pleadings within 21 days after service of the summons and complaint, or, if service was timely waived under Code § 8.01-286.1, within 60 days after the waiver request was sent (90 days if addressed outside Virginia); the only permitted non-answer responses are a demurrer (Virginia’s counterpart to a Rule 12(b)(6) motion), plea, motion to dismiss, motion for a bill of particulars, motion craving oyer, or a written motion raising a preliminary defense under Code § 8.01-276, and each responds only to the counts it addresses. An answer must respond paragraph by paragraph (no general denial), and after an initial response a defendant may not file further demurrers, pleas or motions without leave for good cause. If all demurrers, pleas and motions are overruled, or a sole personal-jurisdiction or process objection is overruled, the answer is due 21 days after that order; if oyer is granted, 21 days after the plaintiff files the documents.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:9","t":"Counterclaims","s":"A defendant may (but need not) plead any cause of action it has against the plaintiff as a counterclaim, related or not and in any amount. It may be filed within 21 days after service (60 or 90 days after a timely waiver request, as in Rule 3:8), or, if a demurrer, plea, motion to dismiss or motion for bill of particulars was timely filed, up to 21 days after the order ruling on all of them; the plaintiff must respond within 21 days after service, and the court may order a separate trial.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:10","t":"Cross-Claims","s":"A defendant may plead against a co-defendant any claim growing out of matters pled in the complaint, including a claim for contribution or indemnity. It may be filed within 21 days after service on the cross-claimant (60 or 90 days after a timely waiver request), the cross-claim defendant must respond within 21 days after service, and the court may order a separate trial.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:11","t":"Reply","s":"If a pleading, motion or affirmative defense sets up new matter and expressly requests a reply, the adverse party must file a reply admitting or denying it within 21 days; without that express request, the new matter is taken as denied. Allegations in a reply are taken as denied.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:12","t":"Joinder of Additional Parties","s":"A person subject to service may be joined if complete relief cannot be given without them or their absence would impair their interest or expose parties to inconsistent obligations; a motion to join may be filed within 21 days after service of the complaint (subject to Rule 1:9). If joinder is not feasible, the court weighs four listed factors to decide whether to proceed or dismiss, and a claimant must plead the names of, and reasons for not joining, such persons.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:13","t":"Third-Party Practice","s":"A defending party may file a third-party complaint against a non-party who may be liable to it for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim without leave if filed within 21 days after serving its original responsive pleading, and otherwise with leave on notice. The plaintiff may assert claims against the third-party defendant from the same transaction or occurrence within 21 days after the third-party complaint is served, the third-party defendant may raise the defendant’s defenses and its own claims, and a plaintiff facing a counterclaim may bring in third parties the same way.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:14","t":"Intervention","s":"A new party may intervene as plaintiff or defendant only by leave of court, to assert a claim or defense germane to the subject matter. All civil rules apply to the intervention pleading except writ tax and clerk’s fees.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:14A","t":"Intervention by the Commonwealth where constitutionality of law challenged","s":"When a party challenges the constitutionality of a Virginia statute, regulation or constitutional provision and no party is represented by the Attorney General, the challenger must promptly file a notice and serve it and the filing on the Attorney General by certified or registered mail or by email to the address in the rule, and the Attorney General may intervene by motion within 30 days after service (extendable). Until that time runs, or if the party fails to give notice within 10 days after written notice of the failure, the court may reject the challenge but may not enter final judgment holding the law unconstitutional.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:15","t":"Statutory Interpleader","s":"Statutory interpleader proceedings follow the Part Three rules to the extent consistent with the governing statutes.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:16","t":"New Parties","s":"On the plaintiff’s motion, served under Rule 1:12 with a proposed amended complaint, the court may add a new party at any stage as justice requires. The amended complaint is deemed filed on the date of the order, Rule 3:4 copy requirements apply, no writ tax, clerk’s fee or cost deposit is required, and defendants must respond as the rules require unless the court orders otherwise.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:17","t":"Substitution of Parties","s":"If a party can no longer sue or defend because of death, disability, felony conviction, removal from office or other cause, a successor in interest may be substituted on motion; if the successor does not consent, the motion and a proposed amended pleading must be served on the successor as original process, and the successor must respond in writing within 21 days after service unless otherwise agreed or ordered. A public officer’s successor is substituted automatically and the action does not abate.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:18","t":"General Provisions as to Pleadings","s":"All written motions, including motions for a bill of particulars and to dismiss, are pleadings. Negligence or contributory negligence may be alleged generally, contributory negligence is not a defense unless pleaded or shown by the plaintiff’s evidence, a limitations defense need not cite the specific statute, and answers, counterclaims, cross-claims, pleas, demurrers, defenses and motions may be combined in one filing if each is separately identified in the caption and body.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:19","t":"Default","s":"A defendant who does not timely file a responsive pleading under Rule 3:8 is in default, is not entitled to notice of further proceedings except notice of the default-judgment motion (counsel of record still gets written notice), and waives jury trial; before judgment the court may allow a late pleading for good cause, conditioned on paying the plaintiff’s extra costs and fees. Except in divorce and annulment, the court enters judgment on the plaintiff’s motion after mailing notice to the defendant’s last known or service address (or a certificate of good-faith efforts), hears evidence on unliquidated damages (a jury fixes them if the plaintiff demands), and lets a participating defendant contest only damages. Within Rule 1:1’s 21 days the court may set aside a default judgment considering the delay, notice and prejudice; after that, only Code §§ 8.01-428 and 8.01-623 apply.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:20","t":"Motion for Summary Judgment","s":"Any party may move for summary judgment once the parties are at issue (not in divorce or annulment); it is granted on the pleadings, pretrial-conference orders and admissions if the movant is entitled to judgment, but not if any material fact is genuinely disputed, and may be partial (for example, liability only). Unlike Federal Rule 56, summary judgment or a motion to strike may not rest in whole or part on discovery depositions unless all parties agree or Code § 8.01-420(B) allows, though depositions and affidavits may be used where the only parties are business entities and $50,000 or more is at issue (Code § 8.01-420(C)).","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:21","t":"Jury Trial of Right","s":"The constitutional and statutory right to jury trial is unchanged, and equitable claims continue to be tried by the judge. A party demands a jury in the complaint or by serving a written demand no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue and filing it with the court; other parties have 10 days after a partial demand to demand other issues, amended pleadings do not reopen the time unless the order says so, and failure to demand waives jury trial absent leave for good cause.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:22","t":"Trial by Jury or by the Court","s":"Issues properly demanded are tried by jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise in writing or on the record, or the court finds no jury right exists; all other issues are tried by the court. The court may seat an advisory jury on equitable claims under Code § 8.01-336(E), must use a binding jury on a plea in an equitable claim under § 8.01-336(D), may use advisory or binding juries by consent, and in mixed cases must sequence trial so the jury decides jury issues and the judge uses its findings.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:22A","t":"Examination of Prospective Trial Jurors (Voir Dire)","s":"After the venire is sworn, the court must ask whether any juror is related to a party, works for a party, has an interest in the outcome, has outside information, has formed an opinion, is biased, or otherwise could not be impartial; counsel then may examine jurors as of right. The court may excuse an unqualified juror on its own motion or on a challenge for cause and call a replacement.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:23","t":"Use of and Proceedings Before a Commissioner in Chancery","s":"A commissioner in chancery may be appointed (including in uncontested divorces) only by agreement of the parties with the court’s concurrence or on a finding of good cause in the individual case. The commissioner sets the first meeting, may compel evidence and examine witnesses under oath, rules on admissibility while recording excluded evidence on request, and files a report of findings and conclusions with exhibits and transcript, giving written notice to counsel and self-represented parties who appeared (with a copy of the report in divorce cases).","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:24","t":"Appeal of Orders of Quarantine or Isolation Regarding Communicable Diseases of Public Health Threat","s":"Appeals of quarantine orders and isolation orders for communicable diseases of public health threat are governed by Code §§ 32.1-48.010 and 32.1-48.013 and related sections. The circuit court must hold such hearings in a way that protects health and safety, for example by phone or video or with protective equipment.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:25","t":"Claims for Attorney Fees","s":"Except for sanctions under Code § 8.01-271.1 and domestic relations fees, a party seeking attorney fees must demand them, and state the basis, in its complaint, counterclaim, cross-claim, third-party pleading or responsive pleading. Failure to do so waives the claim unless leave to amend is granted under Rule 1:8, and on a party’s motion the court must set a pretrial procedure for deciding fee claims.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"3:26","t":"Temporary Restraining Orders and Preliminary Injunctions","s":"Where no statute sets different criteria, a court may issue a brief TRO to preserve the status quo pending a preliminary injunction hearing after adequate notice, or without notice only on sworn specific facts showing immediate irreparable harm plus the movant’s written certification of notice efforts. A preliminary injunction first requires a finding that irreparable harm is more likely than not, then that the claim will more likely than not succeed on credible facts, the balance of hardships favors relief, and relief is not contrary to the public interest; in rare cases of severe harm to the movant and slight harm to the other side, a clear showing of substantial merit can substitute for likely success. Bond (Code §§ 8.01-630 to 8.01-633) and duration (Code § 8.01-624) requirements are unaffected.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:0","t":"Application of Part Four","s":"Part Four applies to civil cases in circuit court, including separate maintenance, divorce, annulment, eminent domain, and habeas corpus or coram nobis proceedings (with limits under Rule 4:1(b)(5)). It does not change how evidence is taken at trial, including the practice of taking evidence ore tenus on legal claims and by deposition on equitable claims.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:1","t":"General Provisions Governing Discovery","s":"Discovery reaches any non-privileged matter relevant to the subject matter of the action, including inadmissible information reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence, subject to court limits for cumulative, available-elsewhere or unduly burdensome requests; the text has no general initial-disclosure requirement, and expert discovery is by interrogatory and deposition (draft reports and most attorney-expert communications are protected), with the eminent domain condemnor paying discovery costs if it initiates discovery. Privilege claims must be described, inadvertently produced material must be sequestered on notice, inaccessible ESI need not be produced absent good cause, and a party expecting ESI production should propose an ESI protocol within 21 days of the request (28 days if served with the complaint) with a good-faith meeting within 15 days if it is not accepted; discovery continues while demurrers and dispositive motions are pending unless the court suspends it. Discovery and protective-order motions require a meet-and-confer certification, responses must be promptly supplemented as to witnesses, experts and materially incorrect answers, and every request, response and objection must be signed (unsigned papers are stricken unless signed within 21 days after the omission is raised), certifies reasonableness and proper purpose, and may not include general or blanket objections.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:2","t":"Depositions Before Action or Pending Appeal","s":"This is the exclusive procedure to perpetuate testimony: a person expecting to be a party may file a verified petition in the circuit court where an expected adverse party resides, serving notice at least 21 days before the hearing (as for a complaint in Virginia or under Code § 8.01-320 outside it), and the court may order depositions if perpetuation may prevent a failure or delay of justice. The petitioner pays the cost (other parties pay their share for witnesses they produce or use), and a similar motion may be made in the trial court while an appeal is pending or the time to appeal has not expired.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:3","t":"Persons Before Whom Depositions May Be Taken","s":"In Virginia, depositions may be taken before anyone authorized to administer oaths; elsewhere in the United States, before any officer authorized locally or a commissioner appointed by the Governor; and abroad, before U.S. diplomatic or consular officers, commissioned U.S. military officers, or local magistrates or notaries. No Governor’s commission is needed, and officers outside Virginia must certify under seal or have their signature authenticated (military officers by their commanding officer).","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:4","t":"Stipulations Regarding Discovery","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, parties may stipulate in writing to take depositions before any person, at any time or place and on any notice, and to modify other discovery procedures including ESI, and may include agreements with non-party witnesses under Code § 8.01-420.4. Stipulations must be filed with the discovery taken under them.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:5","t":"Depositions Upon Oral Examination","s":"Any party may depose any person after the action starts, but the plaintiff needs leave to depose before the defendant’s Rule 3:8 response time expires unless a defendant has noticed a deposition or a certified special notice shows the witness is about to leave; party and party-designee depositions must be taken in the forum county or city, an adjacent one, an agreed place or a court-designated place, and non-parties where they reside, work or do business. Notice must be reasonable and in writing, organizational deponents must designate witnesses after a good-faith conference on topics, only listed persons may attend absent agreement or order, remote depositions are allowed before an officer where the deponent is, objections must be concise and non-suggestive, and instructions not to answer are limited to privilege or work product, court-ordered limits, or a motion to terminate. The witness has 21 days after submission to read and sign, after which the officer signs; transcripts are lodged with the noticing attorney (filed with the clerk only in divorce or annulment cases or when the court directs), and a party who fails to attend its own noticed deposition or to subpoena the witness may be ordered to pay others’ expenses and fees.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:6","t":"Depositions Upon Written Questions","s":"Any party may take a deposition on written questions by serving the questions with a notice naming the deponent and officer (entities designate witnesses under Rule 4:5(b)(6)). Cross questions are due within 21 days after service of the notice and questions, redirect questions within 10 days after cross questions, and recross questions within 10 days after redirect, subject to court change; the officer takes and certifies the answers and the noticing party must promptly give notice of filing.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:6A","t":"Number of Depositions","s":"There is no limit on the number of witnesses a party may depose unless the court orders one for good cause (unlike the federal 10-deposition presumptive limit, the text sets no cap).","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:7","t":"Use of Depositions in Court Proceedings","s":"Depositions may be used for any purpose on equitable claims, for impeachment by any party, and by an adverse party for a party or its officer, director, managing agent or designee; any witness’s deposition may be used on legal claims if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or out of state (unless procured by the offering party), unable to attend, unable to be subpoenaed, a judge, a listed treating health professional, a hospital superintendent more than 30 miles away, a public official kept away by duties, or in exceptional circumstances. Objections to competency and relevance are generally preserved, but form and manner objections are waived if not made at the deposition, notice errors if not promptly objected to in writing, and objections to written questions if not served within the next question period and within 5 days after the last questions. No summary judgment or motion to strike may rest on Rule 4:5 depositions unless Code § 8.01-420 permits, and depositions become part of the record only when offered in evidence.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:7A","t":"Audio-Visual Depositions","s":"Any deposition may be taken by audio-visual means (including video or teleconference) and recorded without a stenographer; the recording is an official record, and any party may make its own stenographic or audio record and get a copy at its own expense. The rule prescribes on-camera opening statements, oaths and identifications, bars editing except by stipulation or order (on a copy only), lets the recording be used wherever a stenographic deposition could be, and requires an appellant to file a written transcript of portions germane to the appeal.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:8","t":"Interrogatories to Parties","s":"Interrogatories may be served on the plaintiff after the action starts and on any other party with or after service of the complaint; answers must restate each question, be under oath, state objections with specificity, and be served within 21 days after service (a defendant gets 28 days after service of the complaint), and are not filed unless the court directs. Contention interrogatories are allowed, business records may be specified in lieu of an answer, and no party may serve more than 30 interrogatories, including all parts and subparts, on any other party without leave for good cause (compare the federal limit of 25).","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:9","t":"Production by Parties of Documents, Electronically Stored Information, and Things; Entry on Land for Inspection and Other Purposes; Production at Trial","s":"A party may request production, inspection, testing or sampling of documents, ESI and things in another party’s control, production of documents to the court at trial, or entry on land, serving the request on the plaintiff after commencement and on others with or after the complaint. The written response is due within 21 days after service (a defendant gets 28 days after service of the complaint), must state objections with specificity and say whether materials are being withheld, and must state the ESI form to be used if none was specified or the requested form is objected to; documents must be produced as kept or organized by request category, and motions require a meet-and-confer certification.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:9A","t":"Production from Non-Parties of Documents, Electronically Stored Information, and Things and Entry on Land for Inspection and Other Purposes; Production at Trial","s":"A subpoena duces tecum to a non-party may be issued by the clerk on a written request served under Rule 1:12, or by an active Virginia State Bar member in a pending civil case (except those excluded by Code § 8.01-407), who must sign it like a pleading and send a copy to the clerk on the day of issuance; if the return time is under 14 days after service, the recipient may serve a written objection that halts compliance until the court orders otherwise. The court on prompt written motion (with a meet-and-confer certification) may quash, modify, shift costs, or direct return to the clerk; inaccessible ESI need not be produced absent good cause; subpoenas to listed high officials and judges need a prior court order; protected health records follow Code § 32.1-127.1:03; the subpoenaing party must share copies on request; and noncompliance is punishable as contempt.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:10","t":"Physical and Mental Examination of Persons","s":"When a party’s (or a person in its custody’s) physical or mental condition is in controversy, the court may on motion for good cause order an examination by health care providers employed by the movant, specifying time, place, manner, scope and examiner and fixing when the report is due. Examiners must be licensed in and reside or have an office in Virginia unless the court allows an out-of-state licensed examiner; the detailed written report must be filed with the clerk before trial and copied to all parties, and an examined party who deposes the examiner waives privilege as to all others who examine the same condition.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:11","t":"Requests for Admission","s":"Requests may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on others with or after the complaint; each matter is deemed admitted unless a signed answer or objection is served within 21 days after service (a defendant need not respond before 28 days after service of the complaint), and lack of knowledge is no excuse without reasonable inquiry. Admissions are conclusive for the pending action only unless the court permits withdrawal, and no party may serve more than 30 requests (including subparts) on another party, other than requests about genuineness of documents, which are unlimited absent a protective order, without agreement of all parties or leave for good cause (to be liberally granted).","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:12","t":"Failure to Make Discovery; Sanctions","s":"A party may move (with a meet-and-confer certification) to compel answers, designations or inspection, evasive or incomplete answers count as failures, and the court must award expenses including attorney fees to the winner of the motion unless the losing position was substantially justified; failure to obey a discovery order can bring orders establishing facts, precluding claims or evidence, striking pleadings, dismissal, default or contempt, plus mandatory expenses. A party who fails to attend its own deposition, answer interrogatories or respond to a production request can be sanctioned without a prior order to compel, and cannot excuse the failure as objectionable discovery unless it sought a protective order; a party who fails to admit a matter later proved must pay the proving costs unless an exception applies. PENDING: by Order of November 9, 2026, effective January 8, 2027, new Rule 4:12(c)(1) will allow the court, on motion and after a hearing opportunity, to bar undisclosed witnesses or information, award expenses, inform the jury, or impose other sanctions when a party fails to supplement under Rule 4:1(e)(1) or (2), unless the failure was substantially justified or harmless; the failure-to-admit provision becomes 4:12(c)(2).","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:13","t":"Pretrial Procedure; Formulating Issues","s":"The court may call counsel to a pretrial conference to consider issues, a discovery plan and limits, amendments, admissions, limits on expert witnesses, reference to a master, preservation and production of ESI, privilege claw-back agreements and use of electronic exhibits. The resulting order controls the course of the action unless modified at trial to prevent manifest injustice.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:14","t":"Disposition of Discovery Material","s":"The clerk may destroy filed discovery material not admitted in evidence one year after the final judgment, or, if appealed, one year after receipt of the mandate or entry of any later final judgment.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"},{"n":"4:15","t":"Motions Practice","s":"Civil motions are set on the court’s motions day or through the clerk or chambers; absent leave, written notice must be served at least seven days before the hearing, after a reasonable effort to confer on the motion and a hearing date, with a meet-and-confer certification. If a supporting brief is five pages or less, the notice and brief must be filed and served at least 14 days before the hearing and any opposition at least seven days before, briefs over five pages get a schedule from the court, no brief may exceed 20 double-spaced pages without leave, and oral argument is heard on request except on motions for reconsideration or where a pro se incarcerated person is counsel of record; papers are deemed served when actually received. PENDING: by Order of November 9, 2026, effective January 8, 2027, new Rule 4:15(b1) will require a notice of motion to disclose any intent to move to close the proceeding, and any other counsel intending to move for closure must file notice at least two days before the hearing.","u":"https://www.vacourts.gov/static/courts/scv/rulesofcourt.pdf"}]});
